  "paper_text": "\\paragraph{Post-hoc power analysis.} We conducted post-hoc power analyses for all McNemar exact tests to characterize the sensitivity of our paired comparisons. For each setting, we report the number of discordant pairs $(b, c)$, the power at the observed effect size, and the minimum detectable effect (MDE) at 80\\% power ($\\alpha=0.05$, two-sided).\n\nFor ack pruning vs.\\ baseline, the Qwen/GSM8K and Gemma/GSM8K comparisons have only 1 discordant pair each, making the McNemar test undefined at $\\alpha=0.05$ --- the near-identical task-level outcomes confirm that pruning acknowledgments preserves accuracy rather than indicating insufficient power. The Llama/GSM8K comparison ($b=3, c=10$, $n=13$ discordant pairs) achieves 39% power at the observed effect (+7.0pp), with an MDE of 9.9pp; achieving 80\\% power would require $n \\geq 28$ discordant pairs. The Qwen/StratQA comparison ($b=18, c=29$, $n=47$) achieves 33% power at +4.4pp (MDE=7.7pp, need $n \\geq 145$ for 80\\% power).\n\nFor ack vs.\\ random pruning, the Llama/GSM8K comparisons (mean $n_{disc}=13.5$ across 10 seeds) achieve 8/10 significant results at $p<0.05$. Qwen/GSM8K comparisons have fewer discordant pairs (mean $n_{disc}=5.3$) due to the high baseline accuracy (91\\%), yielding 1/10 significant seed-level tests; however, the consistent direction (ack $>$ random in all 10 seeds) supports the conclusion via a sign test ($p<0.001$). The StratQA ack vs.\\ random comparisons are fully powered (mean $n_{disc}=133$, all 10/10 seeds $p<0.001$)."
